{"answer": "The critic's clause is correct against the propagation step and I concede that part: a vertex with a 2-to-1 majority at time t is not absorbing, since an earlier flip of one of its same-color neighbors can put it in local minority and it can then flip. Hence SRC_002's claims that an Ω(n) set is 'frozen from step 0' and that freezing monotonically propagates outward are not established, and the abundance argument as stated (constant fraction of initially-agreeing vertices implies permanent freezing) is invalid. However, the clause does not touch the actual obstruction objects the target relies on. A completed obstruction — a coloring in which every vertex has at least two of its three neighbors of its own color — is a fixed point of asynchronous majority dynamics in the strict sense: at such a configuration, updating any single vertex is a no-op, because its two same-color neighbors force it to keep its color, regardless of what any neighbor would do; there is no neighbor in the flipping state the clause requires. The clause therefore defeats only the path to the obstruction, not the obstruction's absorbing status once reached. What remains unproven, and what the critic has not refuted, is reachability and abundance: the target has not shown that the dynamics whp reaches such a saturated matching structure from a uniform random start (its own first countercondition concedes this), and no c^n counting law or simulation evidence is supplied in the artifact. In sum: the per-vertex monotone-freezing mechanism and the Ω(n)-frozen-at-time-zero claim are conceded as unsupported; the fixed-point nature of fully saturated mixed configurations stands; and the headline conclusion (consensus probability → 0) remains unproven by this artifact, though not falsified by the critic either — no simulation or cascade counterexample is cited, and both counterconditions are open."}